"""Original expansion: 100 standard and 100 harder items per subject.
All studies and editorial situations are fictional; no external question source is used.
"""
def extend(add, banks):
    def put(subject, level, skill, prompt, answer, wrong, why, context='', table=None, audit=None):
        add(subject,skill,prompt,answer,wrong,why,context,table)
        q=banks[subject][-1];q['difficulty']=level;q['edition']='expansion-2026-10'
    # Ten original editorial situations, with different evidence, setting and purpose.
    scenes=[
      ('Rhea','a neighborhood repair fair','broken lamps','forty lamps were repaired instead of discarded','volunteers taught visitors to replace damaged cords'),
      ('Cal','a school rain garden','storm drains','runoff reached the drain six minutes later after planting','students measured water flow before and after planting'),
      ('Suri','a traveling sound exhibit','recordings','visitors could compare the same street at dawn and dusk','recordists labeled each clip with its time and location'),
      ('Jonah','a community seed exchange','seed packets','seventy households exchanged seeds during the first weekend','gardeners recorded the source of each seed variety'),
      ('Esme','a tactile map project','raised maps','three visitors navigated the route without spoken directions','designers tested different textures with volunteers'),
      ('Pavel','a school bicycle workshop','bicycles','the average repair time fell from fifty minutes to thirty','experienced mechanics paired with first-time volunteers'),
      ('Tala','a night-sky survey','observations','fainter stars became visible when the nearby lights were shielded','observers compared the same sky region on clear nights'),
      ('Ren','a neighborhood oral-history project','interviews','residents described four former uses of the same building','interviewers compared memories with dated city records'),
      ('Amina','a river litter study','collection bags','the team counted fewer plastic wrappers after bins were installed','surveyors walked the same route every Friday'),
      ('Dario','a low-cost cooling project','test panels','the shaded panel remained five degrees cooler at noon','builders logged surface temperatures at fixed intervals')]
    for k,(name,event,objects,evidence,action) in enumerate(scenes,1):
      def e(level,skill,ctx,p,a,w,why):put('english',level,skill,p,a,w,why,ctx)
      e('standard','Contractions',f'{name} coordinates {event}. The team is certain that ___ ready for opening day.','Which choice correctly completes the sentence?',"it’s",['its',"its’",'it'],'“It’s” means “it is,” which supplies the subject and verb needed before “ready.”')
      e('standard','Relative clauses',f'The volunteers ___ {name} trained will lead the demonstration.','Which choice makes a complete, grammatical sentence?','whom',['whose','which','whoever'],'“Whom” is the object of “trained”: the coordinator trained the volunteers. The clause modifies “volunteers.”')
      e('standard','Count and comparison',f'Because {event} used a reservation system, ___ people waited outside than expected.','Which choice is correct in formal written English?','fewer',['less','fewest','least'],'Use “fewer” with a countable plural noun such as “people”; “than” calls for a comparative form.')
      e('standard','Colon usage',f'{name} requested two supplies for the project___ paper and pencils.','Which punctuation best introduces the list?',':',[ ';',',','no punctuation'],'A colon can introduce a list after the complete clause “'+name+' requested two supplies for the project.”')
      e('standard','Restrictive clauses',f'Only some {objects} were damaged. {name} set aside the ___ needed repairs.','Which choice preserves the intended restriction?',objects+' that',[objects+', which',objects+', that',objects+'; that'],'The clause identifies only the damaged items, so it is essential and begins without a separating comma.')
      e('standard','Comparative form',f'After a trial run of {event}, the revised instructions were ___ than the first draft.','Which choice avoids a faulty comparison?','clearer',['more clearer','most clearest','clearest'],'Use the single comparative “clearer” before “than.” Adding “more” creates a double comparative.')
      e('standard','Past participles',f'{name} had ___ the final report about {event} before the meeting began.','Which verb form is correct?','written',['wrote','write','writing'],'“Had” forms the past perfect with the past participle “written,” not the simple past “wrote.”')
      e('standard','Pronoun clarity',f'{name} showed the new assistant a diagram. The assistant asked whether the diagram could be enlarged.','Which revision of the second sentence is clearest?','The assistant asked whether the diagram could be enlarged.',['They asked whether they could be enlarged.','It asked whether the assistant could be enlarged.','The assistant asked whether this could enlarge them.'],'Repeating the specific noun “diagram” removes uncertainty about what is to be enlarged.')
      e('standard','Relevant detail',f'A paragraph about {event} focuses on the specific procedures used to carry out the project.','Which detail best develops that focus?',action[0].upper()+action[1:]+'.',[name+' prefers to work in the morning.','The meeting room has two windows.','Several volunteers enjoy playing board games.'],'The correct detail describes a concrete project procedure rather than an unrelated personal fact.')
      e('standard','Paragraph summary',f'A report on {event} describes the initial design, a trial with volunteers, and changes made in response to feedback.','Which title best reflects the whole report?','From First Design to Tested Revision',['A Complete History of the Neighborhood','Why Every Trial Must Succeed','The Personal Hobbies of Volunteers'],'The title covers design, testing, and revision without claiming the report addresses unrelated history or universal success.')
      # Harder tasks require scope control, rhetorical purpose, or multiple grammatical constraints.
      e('hard','Scope of conclusions',f'In a pilot of {event}, {evidence}. The report describes only this trial, without random assignment or long-term follow-up.','Which concluding sentence stays within the evidence?','The pilot produced a promising result that merits further testing.',['The project will produce the same result in every neighborhood.','The pilot proves that all alternative approaches are ineffective.','The outcome establishes that no additional data are necessary.'],'One limited pilot supports cautious further testing, not universal effectiveness or claims about every alternative.')
      e('hard','Concession and argument',f'{name} writes: “The trial of {event} was small. ___, its consistent records provide a useful starting point for a larger study.”','Which transition preserves both the limitation and the qualified positive claim?','Even so',['As a result of its small size','For the same reason','In other words'],'“Even so” concedes the small sample while introducing a benefit that remains despite that limitation.')
      e('hard','Paired punctuation',f'{name}’s report contains an interrupting phrase: “The results of {event} ___ although preliminary ___ justify a second trial.”','Which pair correctly sets off the interruption?','comma; comma',['semicolon; comma','colon; semicolon','no punctuation; semicolon'],'The dependent interruption “although preliminary” is nonessential and can be enclosed by paired commas. A semicolon cannot isolate this fragment.')
      e('hard','Agreement across clauses',f'The set of records that the volunteers collected during {event} ___ available, but several entries still ___ review.','Which pair agrees with both subjects?','is; need',['are; need','is; needs','are; needs'],'“Set” is singular and takes “is”; “entries” is plural and takes “need.” Neither intervening plural “records” nor “volunteers” changes the first subject.')
      e('hard','Tense sequence',f'By next Friday, {name} ___ the comparison report, which the committee ___ at its meeting the following Monday.','Which pair best expresses the stated chronology?','will have completed; will discuss',['had completed; discusses','will complete; had discussed','has completed; discussed'],'The future perfect marks completion before a specified future deadline; the later discussion is also in the future.')
      e('hard','Logical comparison',f'{name} compares a pilot team with a second team. Both teams kept records about {event}. “The pilot team’s records are more detailed than ___.”','Which completion makes a parallel comparison?','those of the second team',['the second team','the second team keeps','that of the second team'],'Records must be compared with records. “Those” refers to the plural records of the second team, avoiding a comparison between records and people.')
      e('hard','Sentence placement',f'[1] {name} launched {event}. [2] The first trial exposed a gap in the instructions. [3] The team rewrote that section. [4] A second trial produced fewer requests for help. New sentence: “Several volunteers had interpreted the same step in two different ways.”','Where does the new sentence best explain both the problem and the reason for the revision?','Between sentences 2 and 3',['Before sentence 1','Between sentences 3 and 4','After sentence 4'],'The sentence specifies the gap just mentioned and gives the reason for rewriting the section before the revision is described.')
      e('hard','Function of evidence',f'In a discussion of {event}, the writer states, “{evidence[0].upper()+evidence[1:]}.” The surrounding paragraph argues that observations should guide later design decisions.','What is the most likely function of the quoted detail?','To provide a concrete outcome that can inform a later revision',['To establish that the design works under every condition','To introduce an unrelated historical digression','To show that measurements can replace all practical judgment'],'The concrete observed outcome supports using evidence in subsequent decisions; it does not prove universal effectiveness.')
      e('hard','Subordination and emphasis',f'A writer wants to emphasize that {event} should continue while acknowledging that the first trial was limited.','Which sentence best gives the recommendation main-clause emphasis?','Although the first trial was limited, the project should continue.',['Although the project should continue, the first trial was limited.','The first trial was limited because every project should continue.','The project should continue, the first trial was limited.'],'Placing the limitation in an “although” clause subordinates it. The independent clause gives the recommendation the main emphasis; the last choice is a comma splice.')
      e('hard','Precision and uncertainty',f'The report on {event} finds that {evidence}. No causal mechanism has been tested.','Which revision most accurately states the status of a proposed explanation?','The proposed explanation is consistent with the observation but remains untested.',['The observation proves the proposed mechanism is the only possible explanation.','The mechanism has been verified because it sounds plausible.','The observation is impossible unless the proposed mechanism is correct.'],'Compatibility with one observation does not establish a causal mechanism or rule out other explanations.')
    # Math: ten standard and ten harder families, ten numerical instances each.
    for i in range(1,11):
      def m(level,skill,p,a,w,why):
        put('math',level,skill,p,a,w,why,audit={'answer':str(a),'family':skill,'variant':i})
      n=i+4
      m('standard','Absolute-value equations',f'A sensor’s offset x satisfies |x - {n}| = 3. What is the sum of the two possible offsets?',2*n,[n,2*n+3,2*n-1],f'The offsets are {n-3} and {n+3}; their sum is {2*n}.')
      m('standard','Weighted mean',f'A club has {n} students with a mean of 12 service hours and {2*n} students with a mean of 18 hours. What is the combined mean?',16,[15,18,30],f'Total hours = {n}×12 + {2*n}×18 = {48*n}. Divide by {3*n} students to obtain 16 hours.')
      m('standard','Compound probability',f'A box holds {n} white tokens and {n+2} black tokens. A token is drawn, replaced, and then another is drawn. What is the probability both are white?',f'{n*n}/{(2*n+2)**2}',[f'{n}/{2*n+2}',f'{n*(n-1)}/{(2*n+2)*(2*n+1)}',f'{(n+2)**2}/{(2*n+2)**2}'],'Replacement keeps each draw independent. Multiply the white-token probability by itself.')
      m('standard','Unit conversion',f'A pipe releases {n} liters per minute for 150 seconds. How many liters does it release?',2.5*n,[150*n,60*n,1.5*n],f'150 seconds is 2.5 minutes; {n} × 2.5 = {2.5*n:g} liters.')
      m('standard','Exponential growth',f'A culture begins with {n} cells and doubles every hour. In this model, how many cells are present after 4 hours?',16*n,[8*n,4*n,n+8],f'Four doublings multiply the starting count by 2⁴ = 16, giving {16*n}.')
      m('standard','Triangle angle sums',f'A triangle has angles x°, (x + {n})°, and (x + {2*n})°. What is its smallest angle?',60-n,[60,60+n,180-3*n],f'The angle sum is 3x + {3*n} = 180, so x = {60-n}. The other angles are larger.')
      m('standard','Volume scaling',f'A solid cube has side length {n} cm. A second cube has sides twice as long. What is the ratio of the second cube’s volume to the first cube’s volume?','8:1',['2:1','4:1','16:1'],'Doubling all three dimensions multiplies volume by 2³ = 8; the original side length cancels.')
      m('standard','Function composition',f'For f(x) = 2x + {n} and g(x) = x - 3, what is f(g({n+5}))?',3*n+4,[3*n+7,3*n+10,2*n+4],f'g({n+5}) = {n+2}, then f({n+2}) = 2({n+2}) + {n} = {3*n+4}.')
      m('standard','Median with an unknown',f'The ordered list 2, 5, x, {n+10}, {n+20} has median {n+3}. What is x?',n+3,[5,n+10,n+20],'In an ordered list of five values, the third value is the median. Therefore x equals the stated median.')
      m('standard','Coordinate transformations',f'The point ({n}, -{n+2}) is reflected across the y-axis and then translated up 3 units. What are its final coordinates?',f'(-{n}, {1-n})',[f'({n}, {1-n})',f'(-{n}, {n+5})',f'({n}, {n+2})'],'Reflection across the y-axis reverses the x-coordinate only. Adding 3 to the y-coordinate gives the final point.')
      m('hard','Rational equations and domain',f'For x ≠ {n}, the equation (x² - {n*n})/(x - {n}) = {3*n} has what solution?',2*n,[n,3*n,-2*n],f'Factor the numerator and cancel x - {n} only when x ≠ {n}. Then x + {n} = {3*n}, so x = {2*n}, which is allowed.')
      m('hard','Quadratic discriminant',f'The equation x² - {2*n}x + k = 0 has exactly one distinct real solution. What is k?',n*n,[2*n,n*n-1,4*n*n],f'The discriminant must be zero: ({2*n})² - 4k = 0. Therefore k = {n*n}.')
      m('hard','Inverse functions',f'The function f(x) = (3x - {n})/2 is invertible. What is f⁻¹({n+3})?',n+2,[n+3,3*n+6,n],f'Solve y = (3x - {n})/2 for x: x = (2y + {n})/3. At y = {n+3}, this is {n+2}.')
      m('hard','Conditional probability',f'Of {4*n} students, {n} study both art and music, {n+1} study only art, {n-1} study only music, and {n} study neither. Given that a randomly chosen student studies music, what is the probability that student also studies art?',f'{n}/{2*n-1}',[f'{n}/{4*n}',f'{n}/{2*n+1}',f'{2*n-1}/{4*n}'],f'Restrict the sample space to the {2*n-1} music students; {n} of them also study art.')
      m('hard','Mixture equations',f'How many liters of a 60% solution must be mixed with {n} liters of a 20% solution to obtain a 40% solution?',n,[2*n,n/2,3*n],f'Let v be the added volume. Solute balance gives 0.60v + 0.20({n}) = 0.40(v + {n}); hence 0.20v = {0.2*n:g}, so v = {n}.')
      m('hard','Combined work rates',f'Pump A fills a tank in {3*n} minutes; pump B fills it in {6*n} minutes. Starting empty, how many minutes do the pumps need together?',2*n,[3*n,4*n,9*n],f'Combined rate is 1/{3*n} + 1/{6*n} = 1/{2*n} tank per minute, so the required time is {2*n} minutes.')
      m('hard','Geometric series',f'A grant pays ${n*100} in its first year and half the preceding year’s amount in each following year. What is the total paid in the first four years?',n*187.5,[n*175,n*200,n*150],f'The four multipliers sum to 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8 = 15/8. Multiply ${n*100} by 15/8 to obtain ${n*187.5:g}.')
      m('hard','Logarithmic equations',f'If log₂(x - {n}) + log₂(4) = 5, what is x?',n+8,[n+3,n+32,n-8],f'log₂(4) = 2, so log₂(x - {n}) = 3. Thus x - {n} = 8 and x = {n+8}; the logarithm’s argument is positive.')
      m('hard','Quadratic optimization',f'An open rectangular pen uses {4*n} meters of fence for three sides, with a wall forming the fourth side. What is the maximum possible enclosed area in square meters?',2*n*n,[n*n,4*n*n,8*n*n],f'For width w, length is {4*n} - 2w and area is -2w² + {4*n}w. The vertex occurs at w = {n}, giving length {2*n} and area {2*n*n}.')
      m('hard','Parameter constraints',f'The system 2x + 3y = {n} and 4x + ky = {2*n+1} has no solution. What is k?',6,[3,4,8],f'For parallel distinct lines, the x- and y-coefficient ratios must match: k/3 = 4/2, so k = 6. The constants differ from exact doubling by 1, preventing coincident lines.')
    # Factorial science studies. Each supplies four groups, enabling separate effects and interactions.
    studies=[('Film drying','airflow','heater','dry film mass (g)'),('Water filtration','fine mesh','charcoal','particles removed (count)'),('Compost trial','aeration','added leaves','gas collected (mL)'),('Surface coating','primer','sealant','load before peeling (N)'),('Root culture','nutrient P','nutrient Q','root length (mm)'),('Sound barrier','foam','air gap','sound reduction (relative units)'),('Solar collector','reflector','dark backing','temperature rise (°C)'),('Electrode trial','surface polish','electrolyte additive','current (mA)'),('Adhesive study','surface cleaning','curing lamp','joint strength (N)'),('Water recovery','drainage layer','wick','water collected (mL)')]
    for i,(title,A,B,Y) in enumerate(studies,1):
      base=20+2*i;a=4+i;b=2+i;interaction=2*i+5
      y0=base;ya=base+a;yb=base+b;yab=base+a+b+interaction
      ctx=f'{title}. Investigators tested {A} (factor A) and {B} (factor B). The factors were either absent or present at a fixed setting. All four groups used equal starting samples and the same duration; all other conditions were held constant. Each entry is the mean of five independent trials. Larger values of {Y} indicate a larger response. No model for other settings has been established.'
      table={'headers':['Factors present',Y],'rows':[['Neither',y0],['A only',ya],['B only',yb],['A and B',yab]]}
      def s(level,skill,p,ans,w,why):put('science',level,skill,p,ans,w,why,ctx,table)
      s('standard','Factorial table reading',f'In {title.lower()}, what response was measured with B alone?',yb,[y0,ya,yab],f'The B-only row reports {yb}.')
      s('standard','Reference groups','Which group is the baseline for measuring the effect of adding A when B is absent?','Neither factor',['A only','B only','A and B'],'The neither-factor group lacks both A and B. Comparing it with A only changes just A.')
      s('standard','Single-factor differences','How much does adding A increase the response when B is absent?',a,[b,interaction,a+b],f'Subtract the neither-factor value from A only: {ya} - {y0} = {a}.')
      s('standard','Single-factor differences','How much does adding B increase the response when A is absent?',b,[a,interaction,a+b],f'Subtract the neither-factor value from B only: {yb} - {y0} = {b}.')
      s('standard','Ranking outcomes','Which tested condition gives the largest mean response?','A and B',['Neither','A only','B only'],f'The combined treatment has the largest value, {yab}.')
      s('standard','Controlled comparisons','Which pair isolates B’s effect when A is already present?','A only versus A and B',['Neither versus A and B','A only versus B only','Neither versus A only'],'Both selected groups contain A, and only the combined group contains B. Thus the comparison changes only B.')
      s('standard','Replication units','How many individual trials contributed to the entire four-group table?',20,[4,5,10],'Four groups were each tested five times: 4 × 5 = 20 trials.')
      s('standard','Outcome range','What is the range of the four reported group means?',yab-y0,[yab-ya,ya-y0,yab+y0],f'The range is largest minus smallest: {yab} - {y0} = {yab-y0}.')
      s('standard','Fair comparisons','Why were starting sample amounts equal in every group?','To prevent sample amount from confounding treatment comparisons.',['To force the combined treatment to have the largest response.','To ensure that every individual trial has the same value.','To make a baseline group unnecessary.'],'An additional changing variable could explain response differences; equal sample amounts keep that variable controlled.')
      s('standard','Evidence and magnitude','Which single factor gives the larger increase over baseline when tested alone?','A',['B','They give equal increases.','Neither changes the response.'],f'A increases the response by {a}, compared with {b} for B. Therefore A has the larger isolated increase.')
      s('hard','Interactions','How large is A’s effect when B is present?',a+interaction,[a,b,a+b+interaction],f'Compare the combined group with B only: {yab} - {yb} = {a+interaction}. This is conditional on B being present.')
      s('hard','Interactions','By how much is A’s effect larger with B present than with B absent?',interaction,[a,b,a+interaction],f'A’s effect with B is {yab}-{yb}={a+interaction}; without B it is {ya}-{y0}={a}. Their difference is {interaction}.')
      predicted=ya+yb-y0
      s('hard','Additive model predictions','A model predicts that the two isolated increases simply add. What combined mean does that model predict?',predicted,[yab,ya+yb,yab+interaction+1],f'Add the isolated gains to baseline: {y0} + ({ya}-{y0}) + ({yb}-{y0}) = {predicted}. Do not count baseline twice.')
      s('hard','Model residuals','Observed combined response minus the additive-model prediction equals what?',interaction,[a,b,interaction+a],f'The additive prediction is {predicted}; the observed combined response is {yab}. The residual is {interaction}.')
      s('hard','Comparative ratios','What is the ratio of A’s effect with B present to A’s effect with B absent?',f'{a+interaction}/{a}',[f'{a}/{a+interaction}',f'{yab}/{ya}',f'{a+interaction}/{b}'],f'The conditional effect is {a+interaction} and the isolated effect is {a}, giving ({a+interaction})/({a}). The response ratio is not the effect ratio.')
      s('hard','Necessary assumptions','Which assumption is needed to predict the response with both factors at twice their tested settings?','A response model connecting factor settings to the outcome.',['That five trials were performed per group.','That the table contains exactly four means.','That the largest listed mean is the combined group.'],'The study tested only absence and one fixed setting for each factor. A dose-response relationship is needed to predict untested settings.')
      s('hard','Error and inference','The table does not report trial variability. Which statement is therefore most justified?','The means show a pattern, but statistical uncertainty in the differences cannot be quantified from this table alone.',['Every pair of groups must differ significantly.','All five trial values in each group must equal its mean.','Any interaction must be a measurement error.'],'Means alone do not give the spread of trials. The pattern is descriptive; variability is needed to quantify its statistical uncertainty.')
      s('hard','Confounding and redesign','Suppose the combined group accidentally ran twice as long. What is the best next step before attributing its extra response to an interaction?','Repeat the combined condition at the same duration as the other groups.',['Delete the baseline and keep the combined measurement.','Double the response values for all other groups without testing.','Declare an interaction because the combined mean is largest.'],'Unequal duration creates a competing explanation. A repeat with matched duration directly addresses that confound.')
      s('hard','Symmetry of interactions','How does the change in B’s effect caused by A compare with the change in A’s effect caused by B?','The two changes are equal.',['The first is twice the second.','The first is zero and the second is positive.','Their signs must be opposite.'],f'Both difference-in-differences equal combined - A only - B only + baseline = {interaction}. This equality follows algebraically from the same four values.')
      s('hard','Synthesis of evidence','Which interpretation best matches all four group means?','Both factors increase the response alone, and the combined gain exceeds the sum of the isolated gains.',['Neither factor works alone; only the combination changes the response.','Both factors work alone, but their combination returns the response to baseline.','A raises the response while B lowers it, regardless of the other factor.'],f'Isolated gains are {a} and {b}; their sum is {a+b}. The combined gain is {yab-y0}, which exceeds that sum by {interaction}.')
